  "statement": "You are given a 2D integer array `points` where `points[i] = [x_(i), y_(i)]` represents the coordinates of the `i^(th)` point on the Cartesian plane.\n\nReturn _the number of unique_ _trapezoids_ that can be formed by choosing any four distinct points from `points`.\n\nA **trapezoid** is a convex quadrilateral with **at least one pair** of parallel sides. Two lines are parallel if and only if they have the same slope.\n\n**Example 1:**\n\n**Input:** points = \\[\\[-3,2\\],\\[3,0\\],\\[2,3\\],\\[3,2\\],\\[2,-3\\]\\]\n\n**Output:** 2\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n![](./assets/count-number-of-trapezoids-ii-1.png) ![](./assets/count-number-of-trapezoids-ii-2.png)\n\nThere are two distinct ways to pick four points that form a trapezoid:\n\n*   The points `[-3,2], [2,3], [3,2], [2,-3]` form one trapezoid.\n*   The points `[2,3], [3,2], [3,0], [2,-3]` form another trapezoid.\n\n**Example 2:**\n\n**Input:** points = \\[\\[0,0\\],\\[1,0\\],\\[0,1\\],\\[2,1\\]\\]\n\n**Output:** 1\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n![](./assets/count-number-of-trapezoids-ii-3.png)\n\nThere is only one trapezoid which can be formed.\n\n**Constraints:**\n\n*   `4 <= points.length <= 500`\n*   `–1000 <= x_(i), y_(i) <= 1000`\n*   All points are pairwise distinct.",
  "constraints": [
    "4 <= points.length <= 500",
    "–1000 <= xi, yi <= 1000",
    "All points are pairwise distinct."
  ],
